IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Rules of Court 2021 (Singapore), G.N. No. S 914/2021, current version as at 5 November 2026.
-- ORDER 19 (part roc4), LAYER 1, REGULATIVE FORM: the duties of Order 19 that carry a time limit, as rules a trace can run.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering made by a converter script from Singapore Statutes Online.
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here has been checked against the official text.
--
-- WHY A SECOND FORM.  The decision functions in roc4-order-19-div-2.l4, -div-3.l4 and -div-4.l4 answer "did this party file and serve in time?" for facts the caller gives.
-- This module says the same duties as PARTY ... MUST ... WITHIN ... HENCE ... LEST ... rules, so that a trace can run them as a sequence of events.
-- The decision function is the TWIN of the rule here; each rule below names its twin.
-- A regulative rule can only be traced on the main binary, never asserted: see NOTES.md, "What the main binary does not accept".
-- So the tests (roc4-tests-duties.l4) assert the twins and print the traces, with the outcome written beside each trace from the text.
--
-- TIME.  WITHIN counts in days here: a trace's clock starts at 0 on the day the period runs from, and a week is 7 days.
-- Order 3, rule 3 (not in this part) decides how a day is counted; the traces assume the plain count, and the decision-function twins take its result as an input.
--
-- BREACH is the only failure state a regulative rule has.  Where the text's consequence of missing a time is not a wrong (the appeal is deemed withdrawn, a respondent is barred),
-- the rule ends in BREACH with a BECAUSE that quotes the consequence, and the BECAUSE says so.  No actor, deadline or remedy is added that the text does not state.

-- ===========================================================================
§ `Order 19 -- the duties that carry a time limit`
-- ===========================================================================

-- The parties these provisions address.  They are named for the first provision that uses them.
DECLARE `o 19 duties party` IS ONE OF
    `o 19 r 14(1) the appellant`
    `o 19 r 17(8) the respondent`
    `o 19 r 8(3) the absent party`

-- The things a party does.
DECLARE `o 19 duties act` IS ONE OF
    `o 19 r 14(1) file and serve the notice of appeal`
    `o 19 r 25(1) file and serve the notice of appeal to the appellate Court`
    `o 19 r 22 file a certified copy of the General Division's judgment or order with the Registrar of the State Courts`
    `o 19 r 8(3) file and serve an application to restore the appeal`
    `o 19 r 17(4) file and serve the record of appeal, the appellant's Case, the appellant's core bundle of documents and the appellant's bundle of authorities`
    `o 19 r 17(8) file and serve the respondent's Case, the respondent's core bundle of documents (if necessary) and the respondent's bundle of authorities`
    `o 19 r 17(10) file and serve the appellant's Reply (if any), the second core bundle (if necessary) and the appellant's second bundle of authorities (if any)`
    `o 19 r 41(2) file and serve the record of proceedings, the appellant's written submissions and the appellant's bundle of authorities`
    `o 19 r 41(3) file and serve the respondent's written submissions and the respondent's bundle of authorities`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 8(3) -- the absent party may apply to restore the appeal`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Twin: `o 19 r 8(3) the application to restore the appeal is filed and served in time` (roc4-order-19-div-1.l4).
-- A permission, so a lapse is not a breach: the rule has no LEST, which is FULFILLED on both dialects (a LEST after a MAY is ignored on main and is the lapse on unstable; dialects.md).  The rest of paragraph (3) (the Court may restore; good reason) is a power of the Court and is in the decision function.
-- src:2309 | (3) The Court may restore the appeal for rehearing upon the application of the absent party who must file and serve any such application on all parties who have an interest in the appeal within 14 days after the dismissal or hearing of the appeal and must show good reason for that party’s absence.
@ref O 19 r 8(3) (src:2309)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `o 19 duties party` `o 19 duties act`
`o 19 r 8(3) the absent party may file and serve an application to restore the appeal within 14 days` MEANS
    PARTY `o 19 r 8(3) the absent party`
    MAY `o 19 r 8(3) file and serve an application to restore the appeal`
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE FULFILLED

